/*! \page developer_backend_own_page Ownership Hierarchy

\ref developer_backend_id_page      (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_device_page  (Next) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Each executable program created by the PSCF package is designed 
using a hierarchical system of object ownership. All but one of the 
objects used in a PSCF program is a "child" that is owned by an instance 
of some higher-level "parent" class.  In this system, each "child" object 
is either a member variable of its parent, or a dynamically allocated 
object that is accessed through a pointer that is a member variable of 
the parent object. Each parent is responsible for creating and 
destroying its children.

The Pscf::Rp namespace contains a large number of definitions for class
templates that take the dimension of space, D, and a backend identifer 
class as template parameters. Throughout this namespace, the same pair 
of arguments for these two template parameters are used throughout 
objects in the hierarchy of objects used by a particular executable.
An instance of a class template specialization with a specific pair 
of arguments for D and T may own children that are instances of other 
template specializations with the same values for these two template 
arguments, and may be a child of a parent that is an instance of yet 
another class template specialization with the same arguments of these 
parameters. Construction of the highest level object in this hierarchy 
thus automatically creates a large set of descendant objects that are
all instances of template specializations that take the same values 
for D and T, which are all thus designed to describe systems with the 
same spatial dimension using the same computational backend.  The 
owership hierarchy used by pscf_rp is described briefly below.

\section developer_backend_own_system_sec System Class

The top of the ownership hierarchy used by each pscf_rp executable is an 
instance of a specialization of the Pscf::Rp::System class template, 
which is declared as
\code
   template <int D, class T> class System;
\endcode
The top-level object used by the pscf_rpc executable is an instance of 
a specialization with T=Pscf::CPT, while the top-level object used by 
pscf_rpg is an instance of a specialization with T=Pscf::CUT.  

A value for the integer D is passed to the main pscf_rp program as a 
command line parameter, as the parameter of command line option -d.  
Thus, for example, if the pscf_rpg executable is invoked with a 
command line option "-d 3", as
\code
   pscf_rpg -d 3 -p param -c command
\endcode
then this program constructs an instance of the class 
\code
   Pscf::Rp::System <3, CUT>
\endcode
Member functions of this top-level System object are then invoked to read 
the parameter and command files, which are given in the above example as 
files named "param" and "command". 

An instance of System \<D,T\> with a specific choice for D and T has
children that are instances of the following class template 
specializations:
    - Pscf::Rp::Mixture \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::Domain \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::CFields \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::WFields \<D,T\>

Several of these top-level children are discussed separately below:

<b> Mixture </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::Mixture \<D,T\> owns a set of children that 
each solve the modified diffusion equation (MDE) for particular molecular 
species. Specificially, each "Mixture" object owns an array of instances 
of Pscf::Rp::Polymer \<D,T\>, each of which is associated with a species 
of block polymer, and an array of instances of Pscf::Rp::Solvent \<D,T\>), 
each of which is associated with a solvent species. Each instance of 
Pscf::Rp::Polymer \<D,T\> owns an array of instances of 
Pscf::Rp::Block \<D,T\>, each of which is associated with a particular 
block of that polymer species. Each block object owns two instances of 
Pscf::Rp::Propagator \<D,T\>, each of which owns a data structure that
stores the solution obtained by solving the MDE in either of two possible 
directions within the relevant block.

<b> Domain </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::Domain \<D,T\> owns an instance of 
Pscf::UnitCell \<D,T\> that contains information about the periodic 
simulation unit cell, and an instance of Pscf::Mesh \<D\> that defines the
periodic mesh used for all numerical calculations. This "Domain" object 
also owns owns several other children that contain information about 
space group symmetries and symmetry adapted basis functions, which are 
used in SCFT calculations that search for solutions with a specified 
space group symmetry. 

<b> WFields </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::WFields \<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
chemical potential fields associated with different monomer types. 

<b> CFields </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::CFields \<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
monomer concentration fields associated with different monomer types. 

An instance of System \<D,T\> with a specific choice of values for the 
template parameters D and T thus directly or indirectly owns instances 
of many other class template specializations that all use the same 
values for these two template parameters, and that thus all use the
same backend and the same value for D. 

\section developer_backend_own_field_sec Field Classes

The WFields, CFields, Block and Propagator classes templates all own
children that represent real-valued fields that are defined on the 
nodes of a regular periodic mesh. All such objects are defined to be
instances of specializations of a class template named Pscf::RField,
which is declared as
\code
   template <int D, class T> class RField
\endcode
Specializations of RField with T=Pscf::CUT allocate the underlying 
array of field data in GPU global memory, while specializations with 
T=Pscf::CPT allocate this array in CPU heap memory.  The RField class 
template and several other related array and field templates are 
discussed in more detail on the next two pages.

